  "surprise": "Sigmoid attention (Ramapuram et al. 2024: sigmoid(q.k/sqrt(48) + ALiBi bias - log n) instead of softmax in every head of the six Peri-LN blocks and the MTP block; weights and 9,557,760 params unchanged) on 153-claude scored 555/533/598 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 676/700/685: exact means 562.00 vs 687.00, -125.0, paired -121/-167/-87, every seed far below its counterpart and below the registered -60..+35 range (central -8). Discard. The registered round-1 falsifier fired on every seed: round-1 RL targets solved 498/442/576 (153: 766/747/694, -35%), round-1 sample acc 0.123/0.124/0.171 (153: 0.27/0.28/0.21), round-1 Lean parse failures 17236/17061/14282 of 48,000 = 35.9/35.5/29.8% (100/101 three-seed means ~22%), and the deficit persisted through expert iteration: round-4 RL-solved 1049/963/1064 (153: 1157/1168/1191; metric_rl 1025 vs 1172), whole-run both-ok samples 91,052/84,721/99,081 (sum 274,854 vs ~310-314k for the 136-family runs), eval/round-4 transfer 0.529/0.553/0.562 (153: 0.584/0.599/0.575), so the loss is in the harvest and in the transfer. Surprise 1: pretraining fit was not worse on the next-token metric, only on the greedy one. Final main val loss 0.0851/0.0789/0.0831 (153: 0.088/0.087/0.089, lower on every seed; minima 0.0759/0.0778/0.0819 at 120/240/255 s), train loss 0.051-0.062, mtp_loss tracking main, steps 4033/3902/4011 (153: 4540/4479/4489, -11%, inside the registered 3,800-4,300; 3.5 epochs), yet pretraining held-out greedy fell to 0.936/0.933/0.945 (153: 0.9675 on seed 0; the 136-153 lineage sits at 0.963-0.973). Reading (not established): independent per-key gates improve the calibrated per-token likelihood but flip the argmax/sampled token more often at name positions; with 64 shifted names per lookup, a gate that lets several similar names through at once (the multi-fact retrieval the mechanism was meant to enable) averages their values and no longer identifies one name, which Lean exposes as an unbound-name parse failure, so softmax's competition between keys is doing real work for name binding under the shift augmentation. Surprise 2: bf16 sigmoid attention with the -log n bias was numerically stable (no NaN; the step-1 'train nan' line is the usual unlogged first point), cost only 11% of steps, and sampled at 189-318 samples/s (153: 200-256), so the manual attention is not a decode-speed problem. Eval by length, seed 0 7-12: 91/96/295/58/12/3 vs 153's 95/112/360/83/22/4; solved at >=10 73/62/89 (153: 109/107/107), >=11 15/11/17 (26/22/23), >=12 3/1/3 (4/5/4), L* 11/11/11, max pruned 13/12/12; eval held-out greedy 0.9875/0.9855/0.9875 (153: 0.986), so after RL the greedy gap closed while the sampled-proof gap did not. The deficit is spread over lengths 8-11 and largest in the length-9 bulk (-65 on seed 0), not confined to the frontier. This is another attention-mechanism change below its incumbent (canon 109, sink 117-claude, FoX 117-fable, differential 118, talking heads 123, gated 159-fable2/fable3, SSMax 165-claude crashed) and the first with a lower pretraining val loss and a metric loss above 100, the cleanest case so far of next-token val loss moving opposite to the RL metric. Totals 1941/1617/1773 s (predicted 1,650-2,250). All three pods terminated by run_idea.py (29-35 min, ~$0.85).",
